  {
    "episode_id": 89389,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89389.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 47,
    "turn_text": "you know and and uh... The most important thing is that I think that the voters spoke about what they want in the next Attorney General of California, and I'm humbled that I've been chosen to be the next Attorney General. And the focus for me will be, as we discussed during the campaign, on a number of issues that I think require some leadership that is focused on innovation and not blind adherence to tradition. And so that relates to For example, what we can and should do to reform the criminal justice system in California, which is clearly broken when we see that we have the highest recidivism rate in the country, which means that we have the highest numbers of known offenders that reoffend. In fact, 70% of those who go to state prison reoffend within three years of their release. It's extremely expensive situation in terms of what it costs not only our public safety, but diminishing public resources in a state that is on the verge of bankruptcy. So that's one area of focus for me. And we've actually created models in the work that I've done as D.A. of San Francisco now in my second term that have fixed this problem. Another area of focus for me is what we can do to deal with something that has gone without consequence in many cases, which is the crimes that are of financial nature. and in particular are fraught and that relate specifically to mortgages and foreclosures we have people across the state good hard-working people who you know have been doing everything they were supposed to do to live the american dream warren and and now they're finding their out of work and their predators who have come in in the form of brokers to financial institutions who have stripped the folks of their few remaining assets in their dignity and it is a crime that for the most part is going without consequence because the reality of it, and perhaps a mundane fact of it, is that when we're talking about this level of financial fraud, it's not the Bernie Madoff kind of case. It's instead someone who has lost their home that may have been valued at $150,000, but there are a lot of people who are in the same situation, and they deserve to have a voice and a system. So those are some areas of focus. is what we really have to do in law enforcement and have some leadership around, which is the adoption of technology by law enforcement. Local law enforcement needs more resources to do its job. And I want to be an attorney general that can bring those resources to local law enforcement. Again, just realizing that we have technology now that allows us to communicate more effectively, more efficiently and faster. And communication is one of the strongest tools law enforcement has in our quest to catch the bad guy and make sure that communities are safe. So those are some areas of focus that we've been talking about over the last two years and I believe are very important as we go forward.",
