  {
    "episode_id": 89389,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89389.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 63,
    "turn_text": "Well, I don't know about that. I'm focused on the work and that's what I'm going to pay attention to. I'm very excited about this ability to have an impact on the issues we've been discussing. I'm very excited about the role the Attorney General can play, that is the work that is the statutory and constitutional obligation of the office, but also the work that comes with the bully pulpit to highlight discussions that are about reform and about innovation. I'm very excited about the power. I also see the office can provide in terms of the power to convene on issues such as reentry, bringing together nonprofits and foundations and academicians and academics and law enforcement. leaders uh... it there's so much exciting work that we can do i mean on the issue of technology for example warren you know it some would say oh that's a boring monday topic but let me tell you how it's very real when i was elected the af san francisco in two thousand and four two-thirds of my lawyers did not have email and this is a world-class city that is the first cousin of silicon valley the reality of it is that we are operating with extremely obsolete systems in government and in particular law enforcement and to be able in any way to provide leadership around the adoption of technology i believe will have a huge impact on many live uh... for many reasons one because we will have a government that's more effective and efficient but also one of the crimes that is most prevalent and going absolutely without consequence is a crime of identity theft which is a crime that is is committed because of the the offenders understanding of technologies and the way to use them that will manipulate and can deprive other people of their value and their their their identity but we don't have the infrastructure",
