  {
    "episode_id": 89389,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89389.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 92,
    "turn_text": "It's not just about parole. It's not just about parole. It's about systems reform. Certainly parole and probation as links in the chain of the criminal justice system need much more support and resources. You know, it is in parole and probation departments where we have charged the responsibility of supervising and monitoring known offenders. It is there that we are supposed to ensure that we're knocking on their door from time to time to make sure they're going to that AA class or they're going to the anger management class or they are going to complete their GED. But if the resources aren't there to monitor someone we know is likely to reoffend, then that person will probably end up re-offending. And then we will pay a much higher cost after they've done that, because let me tell you, it cost $10,000 at least every time a prosecutor felony. It cost us $35,000 a year to have someone in the county jail. It cost over $51,000 a year to have someone in the state prison. A reentry initiative like the one I created in San Francisco cost less than $5,000 per participant. And we reduced the reassert rate for that population which we focused on the 18 through 24-year-old population, we reduced the reassents for that population from 54% to less than 10%.",
