  {
    "episode_id": 89389,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89389.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 195,
    "turn_text": "Not nearly rapidly enough. And it's not even clear how much at all. The share of their economy that goes toward consumption has essentially remained stock at around 38%. And I think it's important whenever we're having this discussion, again, to define the terms. We think of consumption as buying another TV or a second car or a third car in many households. That's not what it is in China. The average consumption per person in China is around $2,000, $2,500. We're talking about basics. We're talking about better shelter. We're talking about better communications. We're talking about better clothing. We're talking about better schooling and health care, even though some of that is encountered in the official consumption statistics. And so there isn't a lot of evidence that consumption is rising in China. On the other hand, the fact that it stayed flat is in some ways a bit of good news because the Chinese stimulus program was so much about things other than consumption. It was about things like road building and the building of high speed train lines. The fact that it's remained constant may turn out to be a positive sign. It's just hard to be very optimistic until we see real progress in which China is saving less and consuming more. And as part of that, changing the value of its currency.",
