    "turn_text": "Yes, it is. And there are a whole bunch of different measures of inequality. But in very rough terms, inequality is similar in China to what it is here. It looks different because their poor and their middle class are much, much poorer than our poor and our middle class. And so their rich don't have to be as rich as our rich or there don't have to be as many of them in order to say inequality is similar. But inequality is a really big deal in China, both the official and the unofficial types. The unofficial types take the form that a lot of money remains in the coffers of companies, state-owned companies and private companies. And ultimately that money benefits people and it tends to benefit the high-ranking party officials or executives at those companies, and so my sense is that the official income statistics actually understate inequality in China to a significant degree.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The poor and middle class in China are significantly poorer than their counterparts in the U.S.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Official income statistics in China likely underestimate the true level of inequality.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Inequality is a major issue in China.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "This retained corporate money primarily benefits high-ranking party officials and company executives.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Inequality in China is roughly comparable to inequality in the United States.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "There are two types of inequality in China: official and unofficial.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Because Chinese poor and middle class are poorer, less extreme wealth concentration among the rich is needed to achieve similar levels of inequality.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Unofficial inequality manifests as money retained within companies rather than distributed to workers.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Income statistics reported by Chinese authorities are not fully representative of actual economic disparities.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Measuring inequality solely through official income data may overlook systemic forms of wealth concentration.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Corporate profits being kept within firms rather than paid out as wages indicates hidden wealth accumulation.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "High-ranking party officials and executives have disproportionate access to corporate resources due to their positions.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker has sufficient knowledge or evidence to assess the accuracy of Chinese income statistics.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
