    "turn_idx": 201,
    "turn_text": "well i think that actually from district economic i like that more than the last clip uh... that strikes me as essentially correct that if you look at the history of education it really is the single best predictor of economic growth By no means does it explain everything about economic growth. But when you look at countries that have made incredible economic progress in the last few decades, you see countries that have tended to educate a lot of their people. Like Korea, China, it's no coincidence, has a much more literate population than a lot of other very poor countries. And so what that means is that its workers can more easily follow instructions and be factory workers and figure out how to do things a little bit better. The best example is still this country. I mean, we, about a century ago, there was a big debate, should you send everybody to high school? And much of Europe said no, that's wasting education on the masses. Most people don't need a high school education to function in society. And this country said yes, everybody should have a high school degree. And that has a large amount to do with how much better we've done economically than Europe over the last century. And what I find a little bit worrisome is that we now are conducting a version of that debate within this country. And you hear a lot of people say, well, really not everyone needs a college education. It's wasting it on people who won't be able to use it or who aren't",
